From: afni-win
Subject: [PATCH] windows: path list separator and absolute path test

Why the compat layer cannot handle this: upstream splits directory lists
(PATH, AFNI_ATLAS_PATH, AFNI_PLUGINPATH, AFNI_MODELPATH, AFNI_TSPATH, ...)
by replacing every ':' with a blank, and decides whether a file name is
absolute by testing for a leading '/'. On Windows every absolute path has a
':' after the drive letter, so "C:\afni;D:\atlases" is cut into "C",
"\afni;D", "\atlases", and "-prefix C:/data/out" becomes "./C:/data/out".
The layer could only rewrite the strings (e.g. translate getenv() results to
"/c/..." form), which would leak MSYS2-style paths into every other use of
those variables. The fix is in the parsing itself: mrilib.h defines
THD_PATH_LIST_SEP (';' under _WIN32, ':' otherwise) and THD_IS_ABSPATH()
(also accepts "X:/" and "X:\" under _WIN32), and the call sites listed in
docs/reports/colon-audit.md use them. Behaviour on other systems is
unchanged.

diff --git a/src/1dTrdm.c b/src/1dTrdm.c
index 0427e48..f5311ca 100644
--- a/src/1dTrdm.c
+++ b/src/1dTrdm.c
@@ -302,7 +302,7 @@ static char *path_dir(char *p)
 static char *resolve_path(char *base,char *p)
 {
    char *d,*out ; size_t n ;
-   if(p[0]=='/') return strdup(p) ;
+   if(THD_IS_ABSPATH(p)) return strdup(p) ;
    d=path_dir(base) ; n=strlen(d)+strlen(p)+2 ; out=(char *)malloc(n) ;
    snprintf(out,n,"%s/%s",d,p) ; free(d) ; return out ;
 }
